Well, I have done it at last. After 13 years of playing Nethack (off
and on), and weeks of recent intensive play, I have finally managed to
ascend a character. I almost can't believe it!

A few words about my history of Nethacking: I started playing the game
when I was about seven and my parents had just bought our first
computer. My dad and I played Nethack, trying to see who could get the
furthest. Needless to say, my dad always beat me, because I had very
little grasp of strategy back then (I didn't even know all the
commands!) But neither of us ever got to the end. It was a very
rudimentary version of the game: no altars, no gods, no alignments; no
amulets (besides the AoY), no liches or mind flayers or amphibious
creatures or different colors of dragons, no Gnomish Mines or Sokoban
or Medusa or Gehennom or endgame.... (Just out of curiosity, for
anyone that played the game that long ago: where WAS that darn Amulet
located?!?)
I gave up on the game and picked it up again a few years later. By
this time I had a better grasp of strategy and was able to get to L20
and beyond, but by this time our computer had gotten very old and the
game kept crashing. After a few games lost by "The dungeon collapses!
DYWYPI?" I gave up on it again.
Then last year I thought of Nethack again, and downloaded the current
version. I started playing it and, this time, started reading spoilers
(yes, I am thoroughly spoiled.) And...I have ascended at last!

So...now to the story of Rianna, the lawful human female valkyrie.
Rianna was created far back in the mists of time. Her creator (me)
took her down several levels, then dumped her in the Gnomish Mines and
left her there for several months, having decided that Elven Ranger
was now her role of choice. Rianna felt slightly betrayed, but
proceeded to wait out her long imprisonment, knowing that one day her
creator would give up on those wimpy elves and come back for a *real*
fighter. And her patience paid off; one day when one too many Elven
Rangers had been beaten to death by the hooves of a rothe herd, Rianna
felt her dungeon flare into life once again and knew, with a surge of
excitement, that her journey was about to resume.

So Rianna proceeded down into the Gnomish Mines, encountering only
slight opposition along the way. Around Minetown she turned around and
went back to the main dungeon, suddenly remembering that her
experience was high enough that her god would grant her a powerful
weapon if she dipped her sword in fountains. (I don't usually play
Lawful, so it took me a while to think of this.) To the Oracle level
she went. Dip, dip, dip. "Your sword gets wet. It rusts. It rusts
more. It rusts completely!" Then, after the umpteenth fountain, at
last: Rianna held the long-sought Excalibur and knew that she might
win this one.

So back to the Gnomish Mines Rianna went, quaffing a few fountains
along the way. A grateful djinni gave her a +2 GDSM, which Rianna had
good reason to feel thankful for just a few hundred turns later --
when a monster fired a wand of death at her. Phew! She cleaned out the
bottom of the Gnomish Mines, dragged up all the loot and sold it all
to a gullible shopkeeper, who, it seemed, would buy *anything*. This
bought her a couple of points of protection.

Rianna climbed up out of the Mines and continued along her main
journey. Between her GDSM and Excalibur, nothing could stand before
her. She found a strange staircase leading up to a big place filled
with boulders and suddenly remembered that, months before, she had
come to this place -- and pushed the boulders around such that she was
unable to continue. (Her creator did not know how to solve Sokoban,
and did not like the idea of reading spoilers to figure out how. I
wanted to solve it on my own, but somehow never got around to it. It
always just seemed like a bit of a nuisance.) So Rianna shrugged,
descended the staircase and continued along her journey. A few levels
later she stumbled across a magic portal that led to her beloved
homeland! But what a sight met her eyes! The beautiful land of ice and
snow was under attack by horrible monsters of fire! Many died on
Rianna's sword as she fought her way towards the entrance to the
Shrine of Destiny, but as she finally stood before the Norn, the
ancient figure shook her head sadly and waved Rianna away. "You have
not the experience needed to defeat our enemy. Return when you are a
true warrior."
Rianna bowed her head in shame, but vowed that soon she would return
and vanquish the enemy that threatened her home. She fought her way
back to the portal and re-entered the dungeon.
Deeper she went. Along the way she found the grave of Mysticia, a
wizard that had died many months before. After doing away with
Mysticia's ghost and whatever creature had killed her (hill orcs, I
think) she found to her wonder and joy a huge pile of treasure --
including many rings, wands, and a wondrous bag that...seemed almost
too heavy to lift! Rianna looked inside the bag and saw, to her shock
and dismay, several objects vanish before her eyes! Quickly she pulled
all the loot out of the bag and dropped it in a huge pile. Then she
began looking around for a co-aligned altar, for she had no holy water
as of yet.
So deeper she went into the dungeon. Along the way she found a wand of
wishing that gave her several things she desperately wanted: scrolls
of recharging, a blessed touchstone (to identify all those gems from
the Mines, so she could sell them and buy more protection) a magic
marker, and an amulet of reflection. A few levels later, she obtained
another wish from a grateful djinni. When asked what she wanted,
Rianna hesitated. There were many great and wonderful things that
Rianna knew she could use, but right now holy water seemed like the
highest priority. So (sigh) holy water it was. After dipping a few
potions of ordinary water into the precious potion, Rianna now had
enough holy water to uncurse and then bless the bag. At once it turned
light as a feather. Rianna put all the loot back in the bag and
continued down. Ironically, just a few levels deeper she found *two*
altars to Tyr on the same level! "Oh, well, that's life," she thought.
Some sacrificing at the altars gave her Mjollnir, but she could not
wield it as of yet.
As she went further into the dungeon, Rianna found several more useful
things, including speed boots, water-walking boots, a +5 helmet, and
rings of teleport and polymorph control. After eating a leprechaun,
Rianna was now popping around the dungeon to her heart's content.
(This was to be of considerable help later, in Gehennom.) Finally she
arrived at Medusa's island, and decided she would come back later
(even though she did have water-walking boots.) She had made a vow,
and she would keep it.
Steadily she climbed, back up towards the level where the portal had
stood. Once again, she stood before the Shrine of Destiny. This time,
though, the old Norn bowed her old head in acknowlegment of Rianna's
prowess. "Go now and defeat Lord Surtur."
So down Rianna went, into a hellish netherworld of fire and lava. She
shuddered in fear, for though she could not be harmed by cold of any
time, the touch of fire was still deadly to her. (As I recall, I
hadn't been able to find any source of fire resistance yet! I think I
acquired it somewhere during the Quest, but I'm not sure.) Lord
Surtur, for all his bravado, was vanquished with a single zap of the
deadly wand that had been wielded against Rianna so many levels ago.
Rianna took the precious crystal orb and the bell she found on his
corpse, and journeyed back up to the Shrine. Then back into the
dungeon she went.
Her magic marker gave her many useful scrolls, and she increased her
Excalibur's enchantment to +7, identified almost her whole inventory
and mapped many levels. Among these levels were several containing
hidden rooms of gold! Using her special abilities Rianna jumped into
these rooms and retrieved the gold. She did anger a few guards in the
process, but these were easily avoided. (Later I killed one of them
and realized that...oops...I had lost my telepathy. I played most of
the rest of the game without telepathy; I didn't get it back until the
plane of Air!)
Inside one of these rooms Rianna found a magic portal leading to a
great fortress. After a great deal of hacking and slashing, she stood
before a doorway through which the enemy soldiers poured. There Rianna
stood, and wielded her Excalibur while, one by one, the enemy soldiers
fell before her. After silence had fallen, Rianna began to sort
through the pile of loot. Hmmm...hundreds (literally!) of pieces of
armor, some money, several interesting wands, and tons of food. Not
much that was useful, though. Further she went into the fortress,
along the way losing her beautiful +3 shield to a disintegration blast
from an angry black dragon. The treasure inside, though, was more than
worth it: many thousands of pieces of gold! (Okay, here's one thing
I've always wondered: when your character sees a heap of gold
containing, for example, 28471 pieces of gold, how do they know for
sure *exactly* how many gold pieces are in that pile? Surely they
don't take the time to count them all...) After setting off many
landmines and falling into pits, Rianna had collected all the gold and
gone back to buy protection. AC now about -16. Rianna decided that now
it was time to tackle Medusa's island. As she arrived on the level, a
mind flayer began to float across the water towards her, but a few
zaps from a wand vanquished it. She made her way towards the central
island and, blindfolded, slew Medusa with a few thrusts of her sword.
She then hacked apart the statues, and grabbed the nice shiny silver
shield she found to replace her own lost +3 shield. Taking off her
water-walking boots and storing them in her trusty bag of holding, she
put on her speed boots and went on. (Note this detail: it will become
important later...)
Then down into the next level she went. Suddenly, she stopped dead in
her tracks. She sensed another mind flayer approaching. Quickly, she
pulled out her wands and zapped it...but it made a hole in the floor
and jumped through. Rianna thought about following it, but decided for
the time being that discretion was the better part of valour. However,
when she took the staircase down, she found herself confronting the
mind flayer, which stood before her. Rianna's hand flew to her wands
-- and then, in a sudden surge of rage and bravado, she grasped the
hilt of her sword and held it aloft. "Am I some weak coward, to rely
on magic to slay my enemies?" she cried aloud. "I have vanquished
countless enemies with nought but my trusty Excalibur! Come, vile
creature! I will slay you with my own hands...or perish in the
attempt!"
The creature chuckled mirthlessly, and began to advance on her, its
tentacles waving. Rianna thrust her sword deep into the creature's
body, but it continued toward her, its tentacles grasping at her head.
Rianna shrieked in horror as she felt her brain being sucked out of
her body! Her helmet foiled one of the creature's attempts, but it was
too fast for her. Rianna felt her confidence unravelling. "Perhaps I
should use my wands after all," she thought, and reached for her wand
of death. But then her hand slowed. Which wand was the wand of death?
She had forgotten everything about what it looked like! Then she
remembered: she had stored the wand of death in her back pocket, with
a handy little post-it note stuck to the wand reading: "WAND OF DEATH:
0:2". (Well, something like that... :-)) Quickly she aimed the wand at
the creature, and it screamed and collapsed. She had slain the mind
flayer, but at what cost? She felt as stupid as a lichen. However, she
remembered enough to pull out her unicorn horn and touch it to her
forehead. What a relief! She quickly looked over her possessions and
tried to sort out her memories. She was able to piece together the
identities of almost everything except for her wands, which were
easily enough identified anyway. (This was the first major close call
of the game. My intelligence was down at 4 before I pulled out the
/oD; I think the minimum is 3?)
Another staircase brought her to the castle.  Some fighting (including
another master mind flayer, which she killed from a distance!) brought
her to the front gate. As she stood before the drawbridge, she
realized she had nothing with which to open it. She sighed, and
retraced her steps. She had another close call on Medusa's level, when
she took one confident step out into the water -- and suddenly fell
in! "What?" her panicked mind shrieked. "I was wearing my
water-walking...oops...I guess not." Quickly she pulled herself to dry
land, pulled out the water-walking boots, and noticed to her dismay
that she'd managed to erase almost all her scrolls and dilute almost
all her potions. However, she strapped on the correct pair of boots,
went back up, pulled a horn off the body of some soldier she'd killed,
grabbed a silver saber while she was at it, and went back down. She
figured out the tune in relatively short order, then one by one killed
the inhabitants of the castle. The castle wand gave her an amulet of
reflection, gauntlets of power, and two blessed magic markers. (Maybe
some other stuff, but I can't remember what it was right now.) In the
castle she found many valuable gems and an oilskin cloak. Then Rianna
pulled out her magic markers and began writing on all those blank
scrolls that had resulted from her fall into the water. A few minutes
later, she had +4 or +5 armor, (AC about -30) a +6 silver saber, had
identified all her armor, and had genocided all L's and h's. (No more
mind flayers! Whew!) A moment of panic came when Rianna decided to try
on an unidentified helmet she found, hoping it was a helm of
brilliance...."Your mind oscillates briefly." Oops. A scroll of remove
curse got the helmet off, but then... "AC -20? What happened? I have
all my armor, so...oops. I guess trying on that helmet was a bad
idea." Fortunately, more gold bought back her intrinsic protection
(although it meant another few trips upstairs.) Rianna firmly grasped
her Excalibur in one hand, the silver saber in the other, and went
downstairs to meet the inhabitants of Gehennom.
This was the most uninteresting part of her journey. Gehennom was
boring. REALLY boring. Rianna sometimes thought she might go mad
simply from the unending winding passages and the monotony of the
place. Juiblex provided a bit of a diversion, but a few hits and a
wand of death took him out and a unihorn remedied the sickness (I
didn't know about the wand of digging trick.) Asmodeus was a nuisance,
because he kept popping out of nowhere and casting spells of cold at
Rianna -- which of course did utterly nothing -- and then vanishing
again, before Rianna could do more than hit him a couple of times.
"Show your face and fight like a true warrior, you craven coward!"
Rianna screamed at the empty air, but only an evil laugh answered her.
In the end she shrugged and continued downward (later I came back and
killed him.) Some time later Rianna killed an unidentified invisible
enemy, which later she had reason to assume must have been Baalzebub,
though she didn't know it at the time. Finally, she arrived at Orcus
town, and fortunately got the wand away from him before he'd been able
to zap it more than once. Vlad the Impaler was so easy as to be
utterly boring (honestly, he's a creampuff.) Finally, Rianna arrived
at the bottommost level of Gehennom and found the vibrating square.
Back up to the wizard's tower, found Rodney and killed him, took the
book of the dead, down to Moloch's Sanctum, killed the high priest
(who did nothing more effective than summoning insects). Then up
Rianna went. She'd had the foresight to dig tunnels through the mazes
of Gehennom, and this combined with her teleport ability helped
considerably. She pulled out her stash of teleport scrolls and began
using them, one by one. The mysterious force provided a bit of a
problem around Orcus town, when it seemed determined to keep her in
Orcus town or the three levels directly surrounding it. It got to the
point where Rianna thought she would scream if she ever saw that
stupid level again! But finally the force let her continue, and she
got back out of Gehennom without much further trouble. Quickly she
ascended the dungeon, killing the Wizard of Yendor a few times along
the way. He never stole the Amulet and only attacked directly once,
when he tried to use the touch of death (which failed, of course, due
to her magic resistance.) Along the way she killed a quantum mechanic
and tamed his cat. A few zaps of her polymorph wand gave her a white
dragon named Schrodinger's Cat, which followed happily behind her. At
last she stood before the great staircase that led out of the dungeon.
"Still climb?" Rianna took a deep breath, and proceeded up the
staircase.
She found herself instantly under attack by the Wizard of Yendor and
his friends. However, a few thrusts of her Excalibur took care of
them. She took off her amulet of reflection and put on the Amulet of
Yendor, not wanting to search the whole level square by square for the
portal. She found it in relatively short order. Unfortunately,
Schrodinger's Cat didn't last long (he died about halfway across the
Plane of Earth.) Arriving on the plane of air, she was immediately
confronted with a host of dragons and air elementals. On with the ring
of conflict; off with the AoY, and back on went the AoR. This proved
to be a considerable help when dealing with the dragons and the
lightning bolts, even though it meant that she had to search the whole
level for the portal. However, she found it fairly quickly. The plane
of fire was just long and boring. Rianna took off her ring of conflict
simply because she got so sick of the endless messages: "The fire
elemental attacks the fire vortex! The fire vortex is mildly warmed!"
However, then all the creatures headed for *her* instead, so she put
it back on. With the help of the AoY, the portal was located in fairly
short order, and on into the plane of water she went. At first Rianna
was rather terrified that the bubble would merely shift away and leave
her in the middle of the water, so she spent some time running to try
to keep up with it. At last, however, she realized that if she just
calmed down and stayed put, the bubble would drift and carry her with
it. One false step landed her in the water momentarily (major panic
attack!) but fortunately she was able to pull herself out, having
blanked most of her scrolls (again!) Locating the portal took a while
because that stupid bubble would never go where she wanted it, but in
the end she managed.
As she set foot on the Astral Plane, she heard a deep voice boom out
some cryptic message about "Your desire for conflict will be
satisfied!" "Good," she thought for a moment, "I could use a good
battle." But then, to her horror, she saw several angels of her god,
Tyr, attacking her! "Wait...stop, you fools!" she screamed at them.
"I'm on your side!" Then she realized her mistake and pulled off the
offending ring of conflict. But the damage was done; the angels went
on attacking. So she fought her way out their midst and made her way
towards the leftmost temple, killing Death as she went. Unfortunately,
the temple belonged not to Tyr but to his rival Odin. Despite her
skill in fighting, her hitpoints began to drop alarmingly, so she
slipped the ring of conflict back on again, figuring it couldn't do
any more damage than it had already. Instantly the monsters turned on
one another, and with minimal opposition she made her way towards the
center temple. Pestilence was encountered along with way and
vanquished with a single zap of a wand of death. She strode towards
the temple, pulled off the ring of conflict before entering, ran to
the altar, and laid on it the Amulet of Yendor! Her quest was at an
end.

